---
import { ANSWER_MINUTES, COMPETITORS, RESEARCH_MINUTES, SYNC_TURN_MINUTES } from '../../consts/example';
import { minutes } from '../../lib/format';
import { percent, type TimelineRow } from '../../lib/timeline';
import Card from '../Card.astro';
import RuleList from '../RuleList.astro';
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import Spinner from '../Spinner.astro';
import Timeline from '../Timeline.astro';

const span = (from: number, length: number) => ({
  left: percent(from, SYNC_TURN_MINUTES),
  width: percent(length, SYNC_TURN_MINUTES),
});

const rows: TimelineRow[] = [
  ...COMPETITORS.map((name, i) => ({
    label: `web_research("${name}")`,
    mono: true,
    ...span(i * RESEARCH_MINUTES, RESEARCH_MINUTES),
    text: minutes(RESEARCH_MINUTES),
  })),
  {
    label: 'Write the answer',
    ...span(COMPETITORS.length * RESEARCH_MINUTES, ANSWER_MINUTES),
    text: minutes(ANSWER_MINUTES),
  },
  { label: 'Chat input', highlight: true, ...span(0, SYNC_TURN_MINUTES), text: `locked for ${minutes(SYNC_TURN_MINUTES)}` },
];
---

<Section id="problem" heading="The problem: one slow tool freezes the whole conversation">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    An agent works in a loop: the model thinks, calls a tool, waits for the result, thinks again, calls the next tool, and
    so on until it can answer. Every tool call is a wait, and the waits add up.
  </Fragment>
  <p class="t-body">This is fine when tools take milliseconds. It breaks down as soon as your agent does real work:</p>
  <RuleList>
    <li>
      <strong>Your user stares at a spinner.</strong> A research task that reads twenty web pages, a test suite, a data export,
      a deployment: minutes, not seconds. Nobody can use the chat in the meantime.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work happens one step at a time.</strong> Three independent research jobs run one after another, even though
      nothing stops them from running at the same moment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web requests time out.</strong> PHP and the web servers in front of it often cut a request after 30 or 60
      seconds. A turn that runs longer simply dies, and so does the work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A crash loses everything.</strong> If the process serving the turn stops, the tools it was waiting for stop with
      it, and nobody knows how far they got.
    </li>
  </RuleList>
  <p class="t-body">
    The usual workaround is to build a job queue by hand, invent a way to send the result back to the model, and hope the
    conversation is in a sane state when it arrives. That is a lot of hard, easy-to-get-wrong code, and it is the same code
    in every project.
  </p>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <Card>
      <div class="card-head">
        <h3 class="t-title">One synchronous turn</h3>
        <span class="t-caption muted">{minutes(SYNC_TURN_MINUTES)}, start to answer</span>
      </div>
      <div class="frozen" aria-label="A frozen chat input">
        <Spinner />
        <span>Waiting…</span>
      </div>
      <Timeline total={SYNC_TURN_MINUTES} step={RESEARCH_MINUTES} rows={rows} />
    </Card>
    <p class="t-caption muted">Times are illustrative.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Section>

<style lang="scss">
  .card-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .frozen {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    min-height: var(--size-control);
    padding-inline: var(--space-3);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-compact);
  }
</style>
